{
  "id": "Compound::DB01008 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Busulfan is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hematologic Cancer through multiple indirect pathways, primarily via its role as an antineoplastic agent. The evidence suggests that Busulfan can cause Chronic Phase Chronic Myeloid Leukemia, which is treated by Imatinib, and also causes Aplasia, treated by Idarubicin, and Graft Versus Host Disease, treated by Thiotepa. These pathways all converge on Hematologic Cancer, indicating a broader role of Busulfan in the treatment or development of hematological malignancies. The shared classification of Busulfan and its downstream agents as Antineoplastic Agents or Organooxygen Compounds underscores a common mechanism of action in the context of hematologic disease. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential of Busulfan as a candidate for repurposing in the treatment of Hematologic Cancer, particularly through its ability to influence multiple disease processes that ultimately target the hematopoietic system.",
